.TH RENAME-SIMPLE 1 "2026-09-29" "rename-simple 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rename-simple \- rename files and directories to lowercase ASCII slugs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rename\-simple
[\fB\-f\fR | \fB\-d\fR]
[\fB\-U\fR]
[\fB\-H\fR]
[\fB\-A\fR]
[\fB\-D\fR]
[\fB\-n\fR]
[\fB\-v\fR | \fB\-q\fR]
[\fB\-h\fR]
[\fB\-V\fR]
\fIfiles\fR...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rename\-simple
renames each file or directory given as argument to a lowercase ASCII slug.
Like \fBrename\fR(1), it acts on the arguments themselves, never on the
contents of a directory: select entries with shell globbing, such as
\fI*.jpg\fR, or \fIdir/**/*.pdf\fR with \fBzsh\fR or \fBbash\fR
\fIglobstar\fR. Without arguments, it prints its help.
.PP
A name is converted as follows:
.IP \(bu 2
accented and non-ASCII letters are transliterated to ASCII: Latin
(\[u00E9] \(-> e, \[u00DF] \(-> ss, \[u00C6] \(-> ae), ligatures and
compatibility forms (\[uFB01] \(-> fi, fullwidth letters, superscripts,
\[u2122] \(-> tm), Greek and Cyrillic (\[u039B] \(-> l, \[u0416] \(-> zh);
.IP \(bu 2
letters are lowercased;
.IP \(bu 2
any other character becomes a hyphen;
.IP \(bu 2
consecutive hyphens and underscores are merged into one underscore if the run
contains an underscore, into one hyphen otherwise;
.IP \(bu 2
leading and trailing hyphens and underscores are removed.
.PP
A file extension is kept and lowercased when it has 1 to 10 ASCII letters or
digits (\fIPhoto.JPG\fR \(-> \fIphoto.jpg\fR); otherwise it is converted with
the rest of the name (\fInotes.t\[u00E9]t\fR \(-> \fInotes\-tet\fR). The
compound extensions \fI.tar.gz\fR, \fI.tar.bz2\fR, \fI.tar.xz\fR and
\fI.tar.zst\fR are kept whole.
.PP
Hidden entries (names starting with a dot) are never renamed. A name that is
not valid UTF-8 is reported as an error and left unchanged.
.PP
An existing entry is never overwritten. When the new name is already taken,
or when several arguments would get the same new name, the entry is left
unchanged and an error is reported. The only exception is \fB\-D\fR, for two
distinct regular files with identical content.
.PP
Each renamed entry is printed as an \fB[R]\fR line on standard output, each
deleted duplicate as \fB[W]\fR and each error as \fB[E]\fR on standard error,
followed by a summary line. Marks are coloured when the output is a terminal.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-files\-only\fR
Rename files only; directory arguments are ignored.
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-dirs\-only\fR
Rename directories only; file arguments are ignored.
.TP
\fB\-U\fR, \fB\-\-fix\-unicode\fR
Before renaming, repair mojibake: UTF-8 text wrongly decoded as Latin-1 or
Windows-1252. \fICaf\[u00C3]\[u00A9].txt\fR becomes \fIcafe.txt\fR. Double
mojibake is repaired too. A name that cannot be fully decoded is left as is,
so a correct name is never altered.
.TP
\fB\-H\fR, \fB\-\-fix\-html\fR
Before renaming, strip HTML tags and decode HTML entities.
\fI<b>Tom &amp; Jerry.mp4\fR becomes \fItom\-jerry.mp4\fR. Block tags
(\fI<br>\fR, \fI<p>\fR, \fI<div>\fR, \fI<li>\fR, \fI<h1>\fR to \fI<h6>\fR...)
become a space; inline tags (\fI<b>\fR, \fI<i>\fR, \fI<span>\fR...) are
removed. Named (\fI&eacute;\fR), decimal (\fI&#233;\fR) and hexadecimal
(\fI&#xE9;\fR) entities are decoded; any other text is kept. A decoded slash
(\fI&#47;\fR) is converted like any other character:
\fIReport &#47; Final.txt\fR becomes \fIreport\-final.txt\fR.
.TP
\fB\-A\fR, \fB\-\-fix\-all\fR
Apply all cleanup fixes: \fB\-U\fR and \fB\-H\fR.
.TP
\fB\-D\fR, \fB\-\-delete\-duplicates\fR
When the new name is taken by a distinct regular file with identical content,
compared byte for byte, delete the source instead of reporting an error.
Symbolic links are never compared or deleted. Not implied by \fB\-A\fR.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without renaming or deleting anything.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Also list unchanged entries, as \fB[X]\fR lines.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Print nothing, not even errors.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help and exit.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command line is valid. Errors on individual entries (missing entry,
conflict, I/O error) do not change the exit status.
.TP
.B 2
The command line is invalid, for example an unknown option or \fB\-f\fR
with \fB\-d\fR. Nothing is renamed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Rename a file and every JPEG image of the current directory:
.PP
.RS
\fBrename\-simple\fR "Mon Fichier.txt" *.jpg
.RE
.PP
Preview the changes, unchanged entries included:
.PP
.RS
\fBrename\-simple \-n \-v\fR *
.RE
.PP
Rename the files only, repairing mojibake and HTML markup:
.PP
.RS
\fBrename\-simple \-f \-A\fR ~/Downloads/*
.RE
.PP
Rename every PDF file of a tree (\fBzsh\fR, or \fBbash\fR with
\fIglobstar\fR):
.PP
.RS
\fBrename\-simple\fR ~/Documents/**/*.pdf
.RE
